@* A win rate as a figure, in the tone that says whether it is a good one — the number that
   follows "N×" on every record pill, matchup chip, level breakdown and profile row in the app.

   Nine of these were spelled out by hand and had come apart on all three of the things that make
   them one figure: the weight (semibold in five, medium in four, with no rule telling them apart —
   the aspect chip and the matchup chip are the same control), the tabular figures (one had lost
   them, so its digits jittered as the rate changed), and where the % sat relative to the
   interpolation. The tone was the one part already shared, through WinTone.

   A rate over nothing but drafts is 0 by arithmetic, and WinTone would paint that as a run of
   defeats — so where that can happen the figure is a dash instead. Say so with Completed and this
   draws it; five callers were gating on the count themselves and writing their own dash, in three
   different tones and with the layout classes spelled out twice each. Callers that can only be
   reached with something finished behind them leave Completed alone. WinRateBar makes the same
   distinction the same way. *@

@if (Completed is 0)
{
    <span class="no-figure @Class" title="@Title">—</span>
}
else
{
    <span class="tabular-nums font-semibold @WinTone.ForRate(Value) @Class" title="@Title">@($"{Value:F0}%")@Suffix</span>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The rate itself, 0–100.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public double Value { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Finished games behind the rate. Zero draws the dash; null (the default) says the
    /// caller has already established there is something to report.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int? Completed { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What follows the figure inside the tint, where it reads as a sentence rather than
    /// a column — " wins". Most callers sit after a play count and need nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Suffix { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How the host lays the figure out — the fixed width and right alignment a column
    /// of these needs, in the two places they are a column.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The record behind the rate, for a figure that stands alone with no count beside
    /// it to say how many games it is over.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Title { get; set; }
}
